  – Number of Robots: 1  
  – Justification: A single robot can perform dish retrieval unassisted.

• AT9: Retrieve Dishes with Two Robots  
  – Text: Retrieve dirty dishes using cooperation between two robots.  
  – Relation: AND (part of G1.2.1.2)  
  – Location: Inpatient Room  
  – Number of Robots: 2  
  – Justification: Two robots can effectively retrieve dishes.

• AT10: Retrieve Dishes with Human Assistance  
  – Text: Retrieve dirty dishes with the help of a human.  
  – Relation: AND (part of G1.2.1.3)  
  – Location: Inpatient Room  
  – Number of Robots: 1 (with human assistance)  
  – Justification: Human assistance may be required for dish retrieval.

• AT11: Open Room Door  
  – Text: Open the inpatient room door.  
  – Relation: AND (part of G1.2.2.2)  
  – Location: Inpatient Room  
  – Number of Robots: [1,2] (range)  
  – Justification: Door opening may require cooperation with another robot or human.

──────────────────────────────
3. Summary of Logical Relationships

• G1 → G1.1 and G1.2  
  – Relation: AND  
  – Justification: Both food delivery and dish retrieval are essential and can run in parallel.

• G1.1 → G1.1.1 and G1.1.2  
  – Relation: OR (represented by FALLBACK)  
  – Justification: Either placing food on the table or retrieving it from the tray is sufficient.

• G1.1.2 → G1.1.2.1, G1.1.2.2, G1.1.2.3  
  – Relation: OR (nested FALLBACK)  
  – Justification: Any one of the cooperation alternatives (human or robot) suffices for tray retrieval.

• G1.2 → G1.2.1 and G1.2.2  
  – Relation: Fallback  
  – Justification: Dish retrieval is attempted first; if it fails (e.g. due to a closed door), door opening is triggered.

• G1.2.1 → G1.2.1.1, G1.2.1.2, G1.2.1.3  
  – Relation: OR (nested FALLBACK)  
  – Justification: Any dish retrieval method (unassisted, with two robots, or with human assistance) is acceptable.

• G1.2.2 → G1.2.2.1 and G1.2.2.2  
  – Relation: Sequential (“;”)  
  – Justification: The door status must be checked before opening the door.

• Task-to-Goal Relations:  
  – AT1 is linked to G1.1.1.  
  – AT3, AT4, AT5 run concurrently with whichever alternative is chosen in G1.1.2.  
  – AT6 and AT7 are linked to G1.1.2.2 and G1.1.2.3 respectively.  
  – AT8, AT9, AT10 are linked to G1.2.1.1, G1.2.1.2, and G1.2.1.3 respectively.  
  – AT11 is linked to G1.2.2.2.

──────────────────────────────
Final Note

This goal model captures the essential functionalities of the multi‐robot food delivery system by decomposing the mission into high-level objectives (goals) and concrete operational steps (tasks). The use of OR (via nested FALLBACK) and sequential/parallel annotations reflects the decision points (e.g. choosing between table delivery or tray retrieval, and handling door opening conditionally) and the possibility of concurrent execution where appropriate.

Disclaimer: This design is provided as an example based on the given mission description and should be further validated and adapted to the specific operational context and system capabilities.
